Re: wishing, spelling errors, and wish trees

The problem with the current wishing method seems to be spelling mistakes, 
ambiguities and misunderstandings.  The proposed wish tree method seems to
many to be too helpful.  How about being able to wish for help, and maybe
have some wish-granters be more understanding and helpful than others?  I
could see something like:

nethack:	"What do you want to wish for?"
player:		help
nethack:	"You want to wish for wishing help? [yn]"
	(this is so as not to penalize naive players who maybe don't
	understand wishes)
player:		y
nethack:	"I will help you select something to wish for. --more--"
nethack:	"What kind of object do you wish for? [?!%*...]"
player:		[
nethack:	"The types of armor are: .."
	and the tree continues.

The next time a player wishes (zaps the wand again, or whatever)

nethack:	"You have already indicated a desire for a --more--"
nethack:	"blessed +3 long sword.  Do you wish for this? [yn]"
	If he doesn't, he can start over.

Depending on the wish-granting agent, the tree may vary.  Perhaps the tree
won't ask about blessed/cursed, maybe it won't list *all* of the types of
objects, maybe it won't ask about named objects, maybe it'll include `bad'
objects.  Perhaps this depends on intelligence/wisdom:

nethack:	"You don't fully understand the words.  --more--"
nethack:	"The types of long swords are:"
	(shortened list follows).

If a player chooses not to wish for wishing help, I like the idea of the
parser printing out what it doesn't understand:

player:		blessed +3 dragon scale armor
nethack:	"I don't understand `dragon scale armor'."

Depending on the agent, and how sorely you are trying his patience, he may
try to guess using the various methods proposed on this newsgroup.  If the
player continues to not get it right, he may suggest wishing for help, or 
he may just get fed up, grant some object, and leave.

I think the bit about the parser printing out what it doesn't understand
is very important.  I think the tree could be very helpful, especially for
newcomers to the game.  But the `wishing for help' bit using up a wish will 
make this information cost something, and the restrictions to the tree will
keep the information from all coming out at once.  The rest adds flavor to
the game.

The people who wouldn't want the game to give out such information won't
like this, but I think it important enough to new users to be needed:
How about being able to get such `enlightenment' about kinds of objects
from other sources, say oracles or priests?  This could cost money, or
in the case of priests, sacrafices.  Maybe the sources don't know everything.
(Just how much do priests know about fancy weapons?)  For completeness,
though I don't think it would be strictly useful, you could ask shopkeepers
about such things.  Maybe they'll only talk about what they have in the 
store, or if you bribe them, about other things in their field of
expertise.

I hope my suggestions are useful.

